    "question": {
      "source_id": "unreconciled:step-q181681",
      "problem": "$$\nA={2,0,-2,-4,…},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function defined for each $n\\in N$ as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis a {{field-2}} of $B$ onto $A,$ we have that {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA={-2n+4∣\\,n\\in N}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $⋯$ | $-2n+4$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=-2n+4\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
